# aeneid-003 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Iuppiter et Venus. Fata Romae"
TITLE_EN = "Jupiter Unrolls the Fates — Aeneid I.198–296"
PART = "Jupiter Unrolls the Fates (I.198–296)"
ABOUT = ("On the Libyan shore Aeneas, sick with worry, hides his fear and rallies his men with a short speech; they butcher the stags, roast them, drink, and talk anxiously of the friends still missing. "
         "Then the scene lifts to heaven. Venus, in tears, taxes Jupiter with the plain unfairness of the Trojans' lot: he promised them Rome, and yet Antenor, another Trojan, has long since settled safely in Italy. "
         "Jupiter smiles, kisses her, and answers with the poem's first great prophecy of Roman history: Aeneas's war and his city, Ascanius at Alba Longa, Romulus and Rome, empire without end, and the coming of a Caesar who will close the gates of War. "
         "Virgil's model for the opening speech is Odysseus' encouragement to his crew in Odyssey 12, and the council of the gods that follows is the Iliad's and Odyssey's machinery of divine intervention, retold for Rome.")
U = [
((198,"malorum—"),
"“O comrades—for we are not ignorant, before this, of evils—",
"“Comrades—for hardship is nothing new to us—",
"Aeneas's speech to his men: a rallying speech to a crew after a disaster, rebuilt from Odysseus' address to his men before Scylla in Odyssey 12 (see the next lines). 'socii' — vocative, 'comrades' (allies, companions). 'ignari… malorum' — 'ignorant of evils': 'ignarus' takes the genitive. 'ante' is an adverb here, 'before, earlier'. The dashes mark the aside."),
((199,"finem."),
"O you who have suffered heavier things, a god will give to these also an end.",
"You who have borne worse than this — a god will put an end to these troubles too.",
"'O passi graviora' — 'passi' is the perfect participle of 'patior', vocative plural: 'O you having suffered'. 'graviora' is neuter plural comparative, 'heavier things', used as a noun. 'dabit deus' — 'a god will give': no particular god, just divine providence. 'his' — dative, 'to these (troubles)'. 'quoque' — 'also'."),
((202,"experti:"),
"You both Scylla's fury and the deep-sounding\nrocks approached, you also the Cyclopean stones\nhave tested:",
"You have faced Scylla's fury and the crags that boom from deep within; you have tried the Cyclops' boulders:",
"'Vos et… vos et' — 'you both… you also': the repeated pronoun makes the two sets of dangers equal. 'Scyllaeam rabiem' — Scylla, the sea-monster with barking dogs round her waist, who snatches sailors from passing ships; the 'sounding rocks' are the cliffs of her cave, which boom with the sea. 'sonantis' — Greenough's -is for the accusative plural (you'll see this constantly: 'montis', 'omnis', 'iacentis'). 'accestis' = 'accessistis', a shortened perfect (compare '-ere' for '-erunt'). 'Cyclopea saxa' — the boulders hurled by Polyphemus in Odyssey 9. The whole speech echoes Odyssey 12, where Odysseus tells his crew they have escaped the Cyclops and will one day remember this too."),
((203,"mittite:"),
"call back your spirits, and gloomy fear\nsend away:",
"gather your spirits again and let this heavy dread go:",
"'revocate animos' — 'call your spirits back', as if courage had gone off and must be summoned. 'maestum timorem' — 'sad, gloomy fear'; the adjective is transferred from the men's mood to the fear itself. 'mittite' — 'send away, let go'. Imperatives in short clauses: the style of a commander on the beach."),
((203,"iuvabit."),
"perhaps even this, some day, it will please to remember.",
"Perhaps there will come a day when we are glad to call even this to mind.",
"The most quoted line of the poem. 'forsan' — 'perhaps' (a poetic word, with the verb in the indicative here). 'et haec' — 'even these things', neuter plural: the whole ordeal. 'meminisse' is the perfect infinitive of 'memini', which has present meaning ('to remember'), and it is the subject of the impersonal 'iuvabit' — 'it will be pleasing', literally 'to remember will please'. 'olim' — 'some day', looking forward as well as back. Virgil's source is Odyssey 12, where Odysseus says to his men that he thinks they will remember these troubles also; here Aeneas gives it a more distant, melancholy turn, since his own men will never see the end of their wandering."),
((205,"Latium;"),
"Through various chances, through so many crises of affairs\nwe press on to Latium;",
"Through all these shifting fortunes, through so many perils, we hold our course for Latium;",
"'per varios casus, per tot discrimina rerum' — the repeated 'per' piles up the hardships; 'discrimina rerum' — 'crises of affairs' (a 'discrimen' is a point of decision and danger). 'tendimus in Latium' — we stretch, strain, make for; the first time the goal of the voyage is named as 'Latium', the land around Rome."),
((206,"ostendunt;"),
"seats, where the fates peaceful\nshow;",
"where the fates reveal a place of rest;",
"'sedes… quietas' — 'quiet, settled dwelling-places': 'sedes' is the home Aeneas has been promised (Apollo at Delos, in Book 3, speaks of an 'ancient mother' to be sought). 'ubi' is placed late in its clause. 'fata' — the fates, the sequence of what Jupiter has decreed, a word that will mean more and more as the poem goes on."),
((206,"Troiae."),
"there it is lawful that the kingdoms rise again of Troy.",
"there Troy's kingdom is destined to rise again.",
"'illic fas (est)' — 'there it is right, permitted by divine law': 'fas' is what the gods allow, as opposed to 'ius', human law. The verb 'est' is left out (you'll see this constantly). 'regna resurgere Troiae' — accusative and infinitive after 'fas': 'that the realms of Troy should rise again'. The plural 'regna' is poetic; Troy will rise again as Rome."),
((207,"secundis.”"),
"Endure, and yourselves for favourable circumstances preserve.”",
"Hold on, and keep yourselves safe for better days.”",
"'durate' — imperative, 'harden yourselves, hold out'. 'vosmet' — 'you yourselves', 'vos' strengthened by the ending '-met'. 'rebus secundis' — 'for favourable circumstances' ('secundus' is literally 'following', hence 'favouring, fortunate'). The speech ends on the hope of better times."),
((209,"dolorem."),
"Such things in speech he returns, and with huge cares sick\nhope in his face he feigns, he presses deep in his heart the grief.",
"So he speaks aloud; but sick with enormous cares he wears hope on his face and keeps the deep grief pressed down in his heart.",
"'Talia voce refert' — 'refert' ('brings back') is the verb of speaking, 'replies': the speech is a reply to the men's silent despair. 'voce' — 'in speech, aloud', as opposed to what he feels. The line pair is built on a contrast: voice and face show hope, the heart ('corde') holds grief. 'aeger' — 'sick' with worry, nominative agreeing with 'he'. 'voltu' is Greenough's spelling of 'vultu' (face); you'll see 'volnus', 'divom', 'volgus' in the same style. The leader who hides his fear from his men is a figure Virgil returns to."),
((210,"futuris;"),
"They themselves for the booty gird, and for feasts to come;",
"They gird themselves for the kill and for the feast to come;",
"'Illi' — 'they', his men, the subject of the next five lines. 'se accingunt' — 'gird themselves', i.e. tuck up their clothes for work; 'praedae' and 'dapibus' are datives of purpose, 'for the booty, for the feasts'. 'futuris' — 'for the coming feasts'. The slaughter and cooking of the stags (see the end of aeneid-002) is a typical epic scene, with its fixed steps."),
((211,"nudant;"),
"hides they tear from ribs and entrails they lay bare;",
"they strip the hides from the ribs and lay the entrails bare;",
"'tergora' — hides (an archaic word for skins); 'costis' — dative of separation, used in verse for 'from the ribs'. The sequence of these lines — flaying, cutting, skewering, boiling — is a stock epic feast, the kind Homer describes in great detail."),
((212,"figunt;"),
"part into pieces cut and on spits the quivering fix;",
"some cut the meat in pieces and skewer the quivering flesh on spits;",
"'pars… alii' (next line) — 'part… others': the division of labour. 'in frusta' — 'into pieces'. 'trementia' — 'the quivering (pieces)', neuter plural participle used as a noun: the meat is still twitching. 'veribus' — ablative, 'on spits'."),
((213,"ministrant."),
"on the shore bronze cauldrons set others, and flames supply.",
"others set bronze cauldrons along the shore and tend the fires.",
"'aena' — 'bronze things', i.e. cauldrons (neuter plural adjective as a noun). 'litore' — ablative of place without a preposition, as often in verse. 'flammas ministrant' — 'supply the flames': 'ministrare' means to attend, serve, provide."),
((215,"ferinae."),
"Then with food they restore their strength, and stretched out over the grass\nthey are filled with old Bacchus and with fat game.",
"Then they win back strength with food, and stretched out on the grass they fill themselves with old wine and rich venison.",
"'victu' — ablative of 'victus', 'food'. 'revocant vires' — 'call back strength', as with 'revocate animos' in the speech above. 'fusi' — 'poured out, stretched out', from 'fundo'. 'implentur… Bacchi… ferinae' — 'are filled of old Bacchus and fat game': the genitive follows verbs of filling. 'Bacchus' stands for wine, by metonymy: the god for what he gives. 'veteris' — the wine was given by Acestes in Sicily (see aeneid-002), which makes it old. 'ferina' — the flesh of wild animals (understand 'caro')."),
((219,"vocatos."),
"After hunger was removed by the feast and the tables cleared away,\nthe lost companions in long talk they ask after,\nboth hope and fear between wavering, whether to be living they should believe them,\nor the last things to be suffering and no longer to hear when called.",
"When hunger had been taken away by the feast and the tables cleared, they talk long of the friends they have lost, wavering between hope and fear whether to believe them alive, or enduring the last extremity and deaf now to any call.",
"'Postquam exempta (est) fames epulis' — 'after hunger was removed by the feasting': the verb 'est' is left out. 'mensaeque remotae (sunt)' — the second clause also lacks its verb. 'requirunt' — 'ask after, seek'. 'inter' is placed after its nouns (anastrophe), 'spemque metumque inter': 'between hope and fear'. 'seu… credant, sive…' — 'whether they should believe': a subjunctive of deliberation in an indirect question. 'extrema pati' — 'to suffer the last things', i.e. to be dead. 'nec iam exaudire vocatos' — 'and no longer to hear when called': the ritual call to the dead, the 'conclamatio', at which the friends were last addressed."),
((222,"Cloanthum."),
"Above all pious Aeneas now of keen Orontes,\nnow of Amycus the fall laments, and the cruel with himself\nfate of Lycus, and brave Gyas, and brave Cloanthus.",
"Above all, loyal Aeneas grieves — now for fierce Orontes' fall, now for Amycus's, and in silence for Lycus's cruel fate, and for brave Gyas and brave Cloanthus.",
"'pius Aeneas' — the epithet that marks him: 'pius' is dutiful to gods, family and country (the word is used here again, as in the first part). 'nunc… nunc' — 'now… now'. 'Oronti' is a genitive of the Greek name 'Orontes' (compare the Greek accusative 'Gyan' for Gyas). 'acris' — genitive singular of 'acer', 'keen, fierce'. 'casum gemit' — 'laments the fall of'. 'secum' — 'with himself', that is, silently, in his own mind. 'fata Lyci' — the 'fata' of a man are his lot or end, here his death. The roll-call of names is the same kind Virgil used in the storm; all of these men, apart from Orontes, will turn up alive."),
((226,"regnis."),
"And now an end there was, when Jupiter from highest aether\nlooking down on the sail-winged sea and lying lands\nand shores and wide peoples, thus on the peak of heaven\nstood, and on Libya's realms fixed his eyes.",
"And now it was ending, when Jupiter, looking down from highest heaven over the sail-winged sea, the outspread lands, the coasts and the teeming peoples, stopped on the peak of the sky and fixed his eyes on the kingdoms of Libya.",
"'Et iam finis erat' — 'and now there was an end', of the meal and the talk; the first of the scene's changes. The action jumps to heaven, a fixed move in epic (compare the opening of the Odyssey, and Iliad 24). 'velivolum' — 'sail-flying', a compound of Virgil's own making in the manner of Greek epithets. 'iacentis', 'populos' — 'lying (spread out) lands'; 'iacentis' is again the -is accusative plural. 'sic' — 'in this way', i.e. as he looked; 'vertice caeli' — on the 'peak, top' of heaven. 'defixit lumina' — 'fixed his eyes', 'lumina' being literally 'lights'. The view of the whole world is the place from which the prophecy that follows can be spoken."),
((229,"Venus:"),
"And him, such cares tossing in his breast,\nsadder, and with tears her shining eyes suffused,\naddresses Venus:",
"As he turns such thoughts over in his heart, Venus, sadder than her wont, her shining eyes brimming with tears, speaks to him:",
"'Atque illum… iactantem' — 'and him tossing', the object of 'adloquitur': 'illum' (Jupiter) is the one addressed. 'talis… curas' — 'such cares' are the cares he is turning over, which Venus is about to voice; the line leads straight into her speech. 'tristior' — comparative, 'sadder (than usual)'. 'oculos suffusa' — 'suffused as to her eyes': a participle with an accusative of the part affected, a construction taken from Greek (the 'Greek accusative'); 'nitentis' is again the -is accusative plural, 'shining'. The Venus of epic can be a petitioner as well as a goddess, and here her tears are meant to move the father of the gods."),
((233,"orbis?"),
"“O you who the affairs of men and of gods\nwith eternal commands rule, and with the thunderbolt terrify,\nwhat so great against you to commit could my Aeneas,\nwhat could the Trojans, to whom, so many deaths having suffered,\nthe whole circle of the lands is shut on account of Italy?",
"“You who govern the affairs of men and gods by eternal decree and strike terror with your thunderbolt, what offence so great could my Aeneas commit against you, what could the Trojans, who after so many deaths find the whole round world shut against them because of Italy?",
"Venus's complaint opens in the form of a prayer: 'O qui… regis' — the address of the god in his titles ('the one who rules'). 'res hominumque deumque' — the double '-que… -que' ('both… and', you'll see this constantly), with the genitives of 'homines' and 'dei' (the short form 'deum' of 'deorum'; compare 'divom'). 'imperiis' is ablative of means. 'quid… potuere' — 'what could they (do)': 'potuere' is 'potuerunt' (the '-ere' for '-erunt' again), and 'committere' is understood from the first question. 'quibus' — dative of disadvantage: the world 'is shut to them'. 'tot funera passis' — 'having suffered so many deaths'. 'cunctus ob Italiam… orbis' — the 'orbis terrarum', the circle of lands, closed against them; 'ob' here means 'because of' (Italy, as a destination, being what they are barred from because of the goddess's anger)."),
((237,"vertit?"),
"Surely from here the Romans one day, as the years roll,\nfrom here would be leaders, from the renewed blood of Teucer,\nwho sea, who lands with all dominion would hold,\nyou promised — what purpose, father, has turned you?",
"You promised, surely, that from this stock, in the rolling years, Roman leaders would one day rise, the blood of Teucer called back, to hold sea and land under their whole dominion — what thought has turned you, father?",
"A long sentence in which the main verb is held to the end: 'pollicitus' (the perfect participle of a deponent verb, 'having promised', with 'es' understood) governs an accusative and infinitive, 'Romanos… fore ductores' ('that the Romans would be leaders'; 'fore' = 'futuros esse'). 'certe' — 'surely, at any rate'. 'hinc… hinc' — 'from here (from Aeneas)'. 'volventibus annis' — 'with years rolling': the ablative absolute, a stock epic phrase for the passing of time. 'revocato a sanguine Teucri' — 'from the blood of Teucer called back': Teucer was the first king of Troy, from whom the Trojans are the 'Teucri'; the blood is 'renewed' in Rome. 'qui… tenerent' — relative clause of purpose or characteristic, subjunctive. 'omni dicione' — 'with all dominion, under complete rule'. 'quae te sententia vertit' — 'what judgement has turned you?' — which Jupiter will answer in 260."),
((239,"rependens;"),
"With this indeed the fall of Troy and its sad ruins\nI used to console, fates with contrary fates weighing against them;",
"It was this that eased my grief for Troy's fall and her sorrowful ruins: I would set fate against fate, the later against the earlier;",
"'Hoc' — ablative, 'by this (promise)'. 'equidem' — 'for my part, indeed', emphatic first person. 'solabar' — imperfect, 'I used to console'. 'occasum Troiae' — 'the setting of Troy', as the sun sets: a metaphor for downfall. 'tristis ruinas' — the -is accusative plural again. 'fatis contraria fata rependens' — 'weighing contrary fates against fates': a balance, a bad fate set against a good one, the word 'fata' repeated for sharpness, in two cases next to each other."),
((241,"insequitur."),
"now the same fortune men by so many misfortunes driven\npursues.",
"Now that same ill fortune hounds men already driven through so many disasters.",
"'eadem fortuna' — 'the same fortune', the hostile fortune that destroyed Troy: it has not stopped. 'viros tot casibus actos' — 'men driven by so many misfortunes' (a perfect passive participle, 'actos', agrees with 'viros', the object of the verb in the next word). 'insequitur' — 'pursues', the sense 'harasses' coming from the hunt."),
((241,"laborum?"),
"What end do you give, great king, of toils?",
"What end do you grant to our labours, great king?",
"'quem das finem… laborum' — 'what end do you give of the toils': the genitive of the thing ended. The question picks up Aeneas's own word, 'finem', from line 199 ('dabit deus his quoque finem'); Venus asks the god Aeneas had in mind for the answer. 'rex magne' — a king-title for Jupiter, who is at once king of the gods and the father."),
((246,"sonanti."),
"Antenor was able, from the midst of the Achaeans having slipped away,\nIllyrian gulfs to enter, and the inmost\nrealms of the Liburnians, safe, and the spring to pass beyond of Timavus,\nwhence through nine mouths with vast roar of the mountain\ngoes the sea burst forth and with resounding flood presses the fields.",
"Antenor, slipping out from the midst of the Greeks, could make his way past the Illyrian bays and, safe, through the inmost realms of the Liburnians, and pass the spring of Timavus, where through nine mouths, with a vast roar from the mountain, the sea bursts forth and floods the fields with a booming tide.",
"Antenor, a Trojan elder who in Homer advised the Trojans to return Helen, was in later legend allowed to escape and settle in north-east Italy. 'mediis elapsus Achivis' — 'having slipped out from the midst of the Achaeans' (Greeks). 'potuit… penetrare… superare' — he 'could' enter and pass the Illyrian gulf (the head of the Adriatic), the 'intima regna' of the Liburnians (a people of the Dalmatian coast), and the spring of the Timavus (a river of the north Adriatic, with underground sources). 'tutus' — 'safe', agreeing with Antenor. The line 'it mare proruptum' — 'the sea goes burst forth' — describes the river as an arm of the sea breaking out through the cliff; 'ora novem' — 'nine mouths'. 'pelago… sonanti' — ablative of means, 'with a resounding flood'. A long geographical detour whose point is Venus's grievance: Antenor, a Trojan like Aeneas, did what Aeneas has not been allowed to do."),
((249,"Troia;"),
"Here yet he the city of Patavium and the homes\nof the Teucrians founded, and to the people a name gave, and arms hung up\nTrojan;",
"Yet there he founded the city of Patavium and a home for the Trojans, gave his people a name, and hung up the arms of Troy;",
"'Hic tamen' — 'and yet here', in spite of his dangerous route. 'urbem Patavi' — Patavium (Padua). 'Teucrorum' — an old genitive plural, 'of the Teucrians', here with 'sedes' (the Trojan settlers' homes). 'genti nomen dedit' — he named the people 'Veneti' (Patavium's earlier name), as Virgil's own contemporaries knew. 'arma fixit Troia' — he 'fixed up' Trojan arms: the dedication of weapons on a temple wall to mark the end of war. 'Troia' is an adjective, neuter plural, agreeing with 'arma'."),
((249,"quiescit:"),
"now in peaceful calm laid to rest he reposes:",
"and now, laid to rest in untroubled peace, he sleeps:",
"'compostus' — Greenough's spelling of the perfect participle of 'compono', 'laid to rest, composed', a contracted form of 'compositus' that the verse also uses elsewhere; the word often means 'laid in the tomb'. 'placida pace' — 'in peaceful peace', with the adjective added for emphasis. 'quiescit' — 'rests', a verb for sleep and for death."),
((252,"oris."),
"we, your offspring, to whom you nod heaven's citadel,\nships (unspeakable!) lost, on account of one's anger\nare betrayed and from the Italian shores far are parted.",
"while we, your own children, to whom you promise the citadel of heaven, lose our ships — it is unspeakable — and for one goddess's anger are betrayed and cut off far from the shores of Italy.",
"'nos, tua progenies' — 'we, your offspring': Aeneas is Jupiter's grandson, being Venus's son, and Venus a daughter of the god. 'quibus caeli arcem adnuis' — 'to whom you nod the citadel of heaven', i.e. you grant by nodding assent, the god's gesture; the 'arx' is heaven itself, for the Romans' future deification. The parenthesis '(infandum!)' — 'unspeakable thing!' — is a cry from the heart. 'unius ob iram' — 'on account of the anger of one (Juno)'. 'prodimur' — 'we are handed over, betrayed'. 'Italis… oris' — 'Italian shores', ablative of separation. Venus never names Juno: she is 'one' whose anger is enough."),
((253,"honos?"),
"This the honour of piety?",
"Is this the honour paid to piety?",
"'Hic pietatis honos?' — an exclamation with no verb, 'Is this the honour of piety?' The question is not a request for an answer. 'pietas' is the central virtue of the poem: dutiful loyalty to gods, kin and country, the quality that gives Aeneas his epithet. Venus's argument is that he has been loyal, and fortune has not rewarded it."),
((253,"reponis?”"),
"Thus us to sceptres do you restore?”",
"Is this how you restore us to the throne?”",
"'reponis' — 'put back, restore': the word suggests a kingdom Aeneas is owed (his claim to Troy's throne). 'sceptra' — 'sceptres', for kingly power, in the plural. 'in sceptra' — 'to power'. Her speech ends where it began, on the issue of what Jupiter has promised and what he has done."),
((256,"fatur:"),
"To her smiling, the begetter of men and of gods,\nwith that look with which sky and storms he clears,\nkisses he touched on his daughter, then such things says:",
"The father of men and gods smiled at her, with the face that clears the sky and calms the storms, brushed a kiss on his daughter's lips, and then spoke:",
"'Olli' — an old form of 'illi', 'to her'; Virgil uses it for a solemn tone. 'hominum sator atque deorum' — 'begetter of men and gods', 'sator' being the one who sows, a father in the widest sense; 'deorum' is the ordinary genitive plural beside the older 'divom' (and 'hominum'). 'voltu, quo caelum tempestatesque serenat' — 'with the face with which he clears the sky and the storms': the look that Neptune used on the winds in aeneid-002 (in the simile of the statesman). 'oscula libavit' — 'he touched (sipped) kisses', lightly. 'natae' — dative of the person kissed. 'talia fatur' — 'says such things', the formula that introduces a speech."),
((257,"Cytherea:"),
"“Refrain from fear, Cytherea:",
"“Lay your fear aside, Cytherea:",
"'Parce metu' — 'spare (yourself) fear': 'parco' with the ablative or dative means 'refrain from'. 'Cytherea' — Venus, named for the island of Cythera, where she came ashore from the sea. Jupiter's answer begins with a reassurance, and the whole speech answers Venus point by point."),
((258,"tibi;"),
"remain unmoved of your own\nthe fates for you;",
"your people's fates stand fixed and unmoved for you;",
"'manent immota… fata' — 'remain unmoved, the fates': 'immota' is a neuter plural adjective with 'fata'. 'tuorum' — 'of your own (people)', the genitive plural of 'tui', used as a noun. 'tibi' — dative, 'for you'. This is the answer to Venus's question about 'fata contraria': nothing has changed."),
((260,"Aenean;"),
"you will see the city and the promised of Lavinium\nwalls, and aloft you will bear to the stars of heaven\ngreat-souled Aeneas;",
"you will see the city and the promised walls of Lavinium, and raise great-hearted Aeneas on high to the stars of heaven;",
"'promissa Lavini moenia' — 'the promised walls of Lavinium' (the town Aeneas will found in Latium and name for Lavinia, his future wife, though she has not yet been mentioned): the genitive 'Lavini' is placed in the middle to frame 'promissa… moenia'. 'sublimem' — 'lifted high', a predicate adjective. 'ad sidera' — 'to the stars', the gods' place: Aeneas will be made a god. 'magnanimum' — accusative singular, agreeing with 'Aenean': 'great-souled Aeneas'. 'Aenean' — Greek accusative."),
((260,"vertit."),
"nor me has my purpose turned.",
"and my purpose has not changed.",
"'neque me sententia vertit' — 'nor has my resolve turned me': a direct answer to Venus's 'quae te… sententia vertit?' in line 237, reusing her verb and her noun. 'sententia' — a decision, a view."),
((264,"ponet,"),
"This man for you (I shall speak, for, since this care gnaws you back,\nfurther, and unrolling the secrets of the fates, I shall stir them)\na great war will wage in Italy, and fierce peoples\nwill crush, and customs for the men and walls will set,",
"He — I will speak, since this anxiety gnaws at you, and I shall unroll the secrets of the fates and bring them out at greater length — will fight a mighty war for you in Italy, break fierce nations, and set up laws and walls for his people,",
"'Hic' — 'this man' (Aeneas), the subject of the three verbs 'geret', 'contundet', 'ponet'; it is held back across a long parenthesis. The parenthesis explains why Jupiter will say more than she asked: 'fabor enim' ('I shall speak, for'), 'quando' ('since'), 'haec te cura remordet' ('this care gnaws you again', 're-' marking the repeated bite). 'longius' — 'further than before'. 'volvens' — 'unrolling', the image of a scroll, a record of destiny (the 'fatorum arcana', secret things of the fates). 'movebo' — 'I shall stir, reveal'. 'tibi' — dative of interest, 'for you'. 'bellum… geret' — 'will wage a war' (the war of Books 7–12). 'moresque viris et moenia ponet' — 'will set up customs and walls for his men': he will give laws and a city to a people not yet settled."),
((266,"subactis."),
"until the third summer has seen him reigning in Latium,\nand three winters have passed, the Rutulians having been subdued.",
"until the third summer has seen him reigning in Latium, and three winters have gone by with the Rutulians conquered.",
"'dum… viderit' — 'until the third summer shall have seen him': 'dum' with the future perfect 'viderit' looks forward to a time-limit. 'regnantem' — 'him reigning'. The time-count is three summers and three winters — three years of kingship in Latium. 'Rutulis subactis' — ablative absolute, 'the Rutulians having been subdued': the people of Turnus, Aeneas's enemy in the second half of the poem."),
((271,"Albam."),
"But the boy Ascanius, to whom now the surname Iulus\nis added,—Ilus he was, while the Ilian state stood in kingship,—\nthirty great circles with rolling months\nin rule he will fill, and the kingdom from the seat of Lavinium\nwill transfer, and long Alba with much strength fortify.",
"But the boy Ascanius, who is now given the added name Iulus — he was Ilus while Ilium stood as a kingdom — will fill thirty great circles of rolling months with his rule, will move the kingdom from the seat at Lavinium, and fortify Long Alba with mighty strength.",
"'At puer Ascanius' — Aeneas's son, who becomes a second founder. 'cui nunc cognomen Iulo additur' — 'to whom the surname Iulus is now added': the dative 'Iulo' is the name in agreement with 'cui' (a dative of the name, like 'nomen est mihi'). The aside between the dashes is the play on names: 'Ilus' (his Trojan name, from Ilium) turns into 'Iulus', from which the gens Iulia, Caesar's family, claims descent. 'triginta magnos volvendis mensibus orbis' — 'thirty great circles', i.e. years, 'with months to be rolled' (a gerundive, 'volvendis'): the year as the circle of its months. 'imperio explebit' — 'will fill out with his rule'. 'longam Albam' — Alba Longa, 'Long Alba', the city that will precede Rome."),
((274,"prolem."),
"Here now three hundred whole years it will be reigned\nunder the Hectorean race, until a priestess queen,\nheavy with Mars, twin offspring in birth will give, Ilia.",
"Here, for three hundred full years, the kingship will rest with Hector's line, until a priestess-queen, Ilia, heavy with Mars's child, bears twin sons.",
"'regnabitur' — an impersonal passive: 'it will be reigned', i.e. there will be kingship (you'll see this constantly with intransitive verbs). 'ter centum totos annos' — 'for three hundred entire years'. 'gente sub Hectorea' — 'under the Hectorean race': the line of Aeneas is called Hector's, since the Trojans are the people of Hector. 'regina sacerdos' — Ilia (also Rhea Silvia), a Vestal and royal daughter. 'Marte gravis' — 'heavy with Mars', i.e. pregnant by the god of war. 'geminam partu dabit prolem' — 'will give twin offspring at one birth': Romulus and Remus."),
((277,"dicet."),
"Then in the tawny cover of the wolf his nurse rejoicing\nRomulus will take up the race, and Mavortian\nwalls will found, and Romans from his own name will call.",
"Then Romulus, proud in the tawny hide of the she-wolf who nursed him, will take up the leadership of the people, found the walls of Mars, and call them Romans after his own name.",
"'lupae… nutricis tegmine' — 'in the covering of the nurse she-wolf': Romulus wears the skin of the wolf who suckled him (a legend of the foundation). 'excipiet gentem' — 'will take up the people', as an heir takes up the leadership. 'Mavortia' — 'of Mavors', an old form of 'Mars'; Mars is Romulus's father. 'Romanosque suo de nomine dicet' — 'will call them Romans from his own name': 'Roma' and 'Romulus' share a root, a derivation accepted in antiquity."),
((278,"pono;"),
"To these I neither bounds of things nor times set;",
"For them I set no limits of power and no span of time;",
"'His' — dative, 'for these (Romans)'. 'metas rerum nec tempora' — 'neither bounds of power nor times': a 'meta' is a turning-post in a racecourse, hence a limit. The central claim of Jupiter's speech: there will be neither a spatial nor a temporal limit to Rome."),
((279,"dedi."),
"rule without end I have given.",
"I have given them dominion without end.",
"'imperium sine fine dedi' — 'I have given rule without end'. The perfect tense is a statement of a settled decision: the thing is as good as done. This line is the most quoted of the speech and the 'imperium' promised is often called 'imperium sine fine', the ideology of Augustan Rome."),
((282,"togatam:"),
"Nay more, harsh Juno,\nwho now sea and lands and sky with fear wearies,\nher counsels to the better will bring back, and with me will cherish\nthe Romans, lords of affairs, and the toga-clad race:",
"Why, even harsh Juno, who now wears out sea and land and sky with her dread, will turn her purposes to kinder ones, and join me in cherishing the Romans, masters of the world, the people of the toga:",
"'Quin' — 'nay more, indeed', adding a further point. 'aspera Iuno' — Juno is 'harsh', as in the opening lines of the poem. 'metu… fatigat' — 'wearies with fear': she is the one frightening sea, land and sky. 'consilia in melius referet' — 'will bring her plans back to something better'. 'mecumque fovebit' — 'and will cherish with me'. 'rerum dominos' — 'lords of affairs', i.e. rulers of the world. 'gentem togatam' — 'the toga-wearing people': the toga is the Roman civilian dress, a mark of peace and citizenship."),
((283,"placitum."),
"so it has been decided.",
"So it has been resolved.",
"'sic placitum (est)' — 'thus it has pleased', 'thus it is decided': 'placitum' is the participle of 'placeo', used impersonally, with 'est' left out. The phrase marks a decree of Jupiter, which is also the voice of fate."),
((285,"Argis."),
"There will come, as the lustra slip by, an age,\nwhen the house of Assaracus Phthia and famed Mycenae\nwith servitude will press, and over conquered Argos will rule.",
"As the years glide past, an age will come when the house of Assaracus will crush Phthia and famed Mycenae under servitude, and hold lordship over conquered Argos.",
"'lustris labentibus' — 'with lustra gliding by' (ablative absolute): a 'lustrum' is a five-year period, the interval at which Romans took the census and purified the city. 'domus Assaraci' — the house of Assaracus, a Trojan king, ancestor of Aeneas, so the Trojans' descendants, the Romans. 'Phthiam clarasque Mycenas' — Phthia was Achilles' home, Mycenae Agamemnon's: the homelands of Troy's destroyers. 'servitio premet' — 'will press with slavery'. 'victis dominabitur Argis' — 'will rule over conquered Argos' (dative with 'dominor'); Rome's conquest of Greece in the second century BC, set as the reversal of the Trojan War."),
((288,"Iulo."),
"There will be born, of fair origin, a Trojan Caesar,\nwho shall bound his empire with Ocean, his fame with the stars,—\nJulius, a name handed down from great Iulus.",
"A Trojan Caesar will be born of splendid stock, to bound his empire with Ocean and his fame with the stars — Julius, a name passed down from great Iulus.",
"'Troianus Caesar' — the Caesar of the line of Troy; which Caesar is left open, and the identity (Augustus, whom Virgil's first readers took it to be, or Julius Caesar) has been disputed since. 'qui terminet' — 'who shall bound': the relative clause with the subjunctive gives purpose or character. 'imperium oceano, famam astris' — a pair of boundaries, empire's limit at Ocean (the stream encircling the world), fame's at the stars; compare 'ad sidera' in 259. 'Iulius… demissum nomen Iulo' — 'Julius, a name sent down from great Iulus', the connection of the Julian family with Ascanius."),
((290,"secura;"),
"Him you one day in heaven, with the spoils of the East laden,\nwill receive, free of care;",
"You, no longer afraid, will one day welcome him into heaven, laden with the spoils of the East;",
"'Hunc' — 'this man' (the Caesar), emphatic first word. 'olim' — 'some day', looking far ahead. 'spoliis Orientis onustum' — 'laden with the spoils of the East' (a reference to the victories in Asia and Egypt). 'secura' — 'free from care', agreeing with 'tu' (Venus). The Caesar is to be received into heaven, an apotheosis, like Aeneas."),
((290,"votis."),
"he will be called upon in vows also, this one.",
"he too will be invoked in prayers.",
"'vocabitur… votis' — 'will be called by vows', i.e. he will receive prayers (and so will be a god). 'hic quoque' — 'this one too', as Aeneas is to join the gods."),
((291,"bellis;"),
"Harsh then, wars having been laid aside, will grow mild the ages;",
"Then wars will be laid aside, and the harsh ages will grow gentle;",
"'Aspera… saecula' — 'the harsh ages' (the age that was hard with war), the subject of 'mitescent'. 'positis bellis' — 'wars having been put down', ablative absolute. The word order puts 'aspera' first and 'bellis' last to frame the line. The Augustan peace is presented as the end of ages of fighting."),
((293,"dabunt;"),
"grey Faith, and Vesta, with brother Remus Quirinus,\nlaws will give;",
"white-haired Faith and Vesta, and Quirinus with his brother Remus, will give the law;",
"'cana Fides' — 'hoary Faith', Good Faith as a personified goddess, grey-haired as the ancient emblem of trust. 'Vesta' — goddess of the hearth. 'Remo cum fratre Quirinus' — 'Quirinus with his brother Remus': Quirinus is the deified Romulus; the brothers are reconciled after the old fratricide. 'iura dabunt' — 'will give laws', the subject being all of those named."),
((294,"portae;"),
"dread with iron and with tight bonds\nwill be closed the gates of War;",
"the dread gates of War will be shut with iron and close-fitting bars;",
"'dirae… Belli portae' — 'the dread gates of War', framing the clause; 'Bellum' is personified. The Romans kept the gates of the temple of Janus open in wartime and shut them in peace; Augustus shut them in 29 BC. 'ferro et compagibus artis' — 'with iron and tight joints'. 'claudentur' — passive future."),
((296,"cruento.”"),
"Impious Frenzy within,\nsitting on savage arms, and, bound with a hundred bronze\nknots behind the back, will roar, bristling, with bloody mouth.”",
"and inside, impious Frenzy, sitting on a heap of savage weapons, his hands bound behind him with a hundred knots of bronze, will roar, grisly, from a bloody mouth.”",
"'Furor impius' — Frenzy (the madness of war) as a personified figure, shut up inside the temple. 'saeva sedens super arma' — 'sitting on savage arms', a sound-pattern with 's'. 'centum vinctus aenis post tergum nodis' — 'bound with a hundred bronze knots behind the back', the adjective 'aenis' and noun 'nodis' enclosing 'post tergum'. 'fremet horridus ore cruento' — 'will roar, bristling, with bloody mouth'. The closing quotation mark ends Jupiter's speech, which is also the poem's largest single statement of its theme: war overcome and bound by Roman peace."),
]
